# Test Postgres and Redis

Throwaway services on the homelab cluster for tests that need a real Postgres
(IMM-2039) or Redis (IMM-2040). Namespace `[[ test_namespace ]]`, reachable from the
LAN and from in-cluster CI through NodePorts on any node, for example
`192.168.0.10`.

| Service | Image | NodePort |
|---|---|---|
| Postgres (db `[[ ident ]]_test`, user `[[ ident ]]`) | `postgres:18.6-alpine` | [[ test_postgres_nodeport ]] |
| Redis (password required, no persistence) | `redis:8.10-alpine` | [[ test_redis_nodeport ]] |

There are no persistent volumes. A pod restart wipes everything, so a test
creates what it needs (its own schema or key prefix) and never relies on data
left by an earlier run.

## Connecting from tests

Tests read three environment variables. A test that needs one of them and finds
it unset fails rather than skips, because a skipped check proves nothing.

The one deliberate exception is `[[ env_prefix ]]_SKIP_SERVICE_TESTS=1`, which
skips every test that needs these services. It exists for a checkout with no
test services, such as the go-web-base template's own CI. This project's
pipeline never sets it.

```sh
PW=$(kubectl get secret test-services -n [[ test_namespace ]] -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d)
export [[ env_prefix ]]_TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgres://[[ ident ]]:$PW@192.168.0.10:[[ test_postgres_nodeport ]]/[[ ident ]]_test?sslmode=disable"
export [[ env_prefix ]]_TEST_REDIS_ADDR=192.168.0.10:[[ test_redis_nodeport ]]
export [[ env_prefix ]]_TEST_REDIS_PASSWORD="$PW"
```

## Applying

The first time, create the namespace and seal a generated password (hex, so
it needs no escaping in the DSN). The plaintext only exists in the pipe:

```sh
kubectl apply -f namespace.yaml
kubectl create secret generic test-services -n [[ test_namespace ]] \
  --from-literal=password="$(openssl rand -hex 24)" \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml \
| kubeseal --controller-namespace kube-system \
    --controller-name sealed-secrets-controller -o yaml \
> test-services-sealed.yaml
```

Commit `test-services-sealed.yaml` with a header comment recording the date
and that the controller unsealed it. Then:

```sh
kubectl apply -f namespace.yaml -f test-services-sealed.yaml -f postgres.yaml -f redis.yaml
```

The password lives only in the SealedSecret. To rotate it, reseal a new value
over `test-services-sealed.yaml` and restart both deployments.
